able_display runs on RasPi2 inside the ABLE black box with four 7-segment displays.
able_fr24 also runs on the Black Box and fetches data from FlightRadar24.
able_live runs on RasPi3b an fetches data from PilotAware.

able_display calls get_able_data.py to read data from two sources:
  It reads the data from /mem/fr24_data
  It uses Tailscale to read the data from file able-live:/mem/pilotaware_data

It relies on able_fr24 which runs on this host under service able_fr24. This writes the data file.
Note that able-live writes file /mem/pilotaware_data and makes it available via Tailscale.

Note that able_fr24 also writes file /mem/fr24_live_data and makes it available via Tailscale so it
can be read by able-live.

Host            Filename                    Data Format     Written By      Read By
----            --------                    -----------     ----------      -------
able-display    /mem/fr24_data              --,02,--,--     able_fr24.py    able_display,autopilot
able-display    /mem/fr24_live_data         #1,51.234,      able_fr24.py    able_live
able-live       /mem/pilotaware_data        --,02,--,--     able_live       able_display,autopilot

To Create RAM Disk:

sudo mkdir /mem
vi /etc/fstab
  tmpfs /mem tmpfs nodev,nosuid,size=1M,nofail 0 0

Radiotephony Feature Added
--------------------------
Hardware PTT switch wired up and connected to G-NIUS secondary PC via USB hub behind radio stack. Had to install
joy2key.exe as the PTT button wasn't being detected properly in Teamspeak so mapped it to 'p' key.

Headset sockets added inside Sim and connect via USB through firewall and directly into back of secondary PC (failed
to work properly when connected via the USB hub).

Classroom PC runs radiotelephony.exe with arg = SIM. This starts a Teamspeak server and a Teamspeak client. The
client connects to the server using address 127.0.0.1. It also sends its own IP to able-live2 (UDP to gnius-live
process). It gets the IP address of able-live2 using Tailscale (/mem/able-live.ip). It receives aircraft location
+ RT data from the gnius-live process running on able-live2. RT data is displayed in the RT GUI and aircraft location
is written to a whazzup.txt file that is read by Little Navmap.

G-NIUS Sim main PC runs aerobility_data_link.exe which sends aircraft location + RT data to able-live2 (UDP to
gnius-live process). It gets the IP address of able-live2 using Tailscale (/mem/able-live.ip).

G-EFIS Sim PC runs g1000_data_link.exe which sends aircraft location to able-live2 (UDP to able-live process).
It gets the IP address of able-live2 using Tailscale (/mem/able-live.ip).

G-NIUS Sim Secondary PC runs a Teamspeak client which connects to the Teamspeak server running on Classroom PC. It gets
the IP address of the classroom PC using Tailscale (/mem/teamspeak.ip). The sim headset connects to this Teamspeak.

Host able-live2 (RasPi4) runs gnius-live and able-live. The gnius-live process receives data from the G-NIUS sim and
forwards it to both able-live (127.0.0.1) and the classroom PC (gets IP from Tailscale /mem/teamspeak.ip). It also
receives the IP address of the classroom PC via UDP and writes it to the local /mem/teamspeak.ip file.
The able-live process receives data from the G-NIUS sim (forwarded by gnius-live), the G-EFIS sim (directly),
flightradar24 and pilotaware (running on the Raspberry Pi in Al's office). The able-live process displays all the
aircraft data on the map showing on the large screen in the classroom.

Host able-live2 also runs /home/pi/able-live/init.sh at reboot to write its own IP address to /mem/able-live.ip
